# Invariants of a production build, in one place.
#
# CI, docker-compose and a developer checking the bundle by hand used to each spell
# these out separately, so "I built with VITE_USE_DEMO_DATA=false" did not mean the
# artifact matched what CI produced. Vite loads this file for every production-mode
# build, so the three paths cannot drift.
#
# Everything a production frontend build needs now lives here, including the Microsoft
# and API values that used to be build arguments passed in from the deployment host.
# That move is what makes a frontend image reproducible from a commit: a build argument
# supplied at deploy time meant the same commit could produce different bundles, which
# is precisely what H10 exists to stop. Changing any value below requires a pull
# request, so a commit and a bundle correspond one to one.
#
# All four are public identifiers. The Microsoft application and directory ids appear in
# every sign-in request and the redirect URI is visible in the browser; they are not
# secrets and they end up readable in the shipped bundle either way. Never put a secret
# in this file: it is committed and it ends up in the bundle.
#
# VITE_MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI must match the redirect URI registered in Entra byte for
# byte -- https://evts.id.vn and https://evts.id.vn/ are different URIs there, and a
# mismatch fails at sign-in with AADSTS50011, not at build time.
#
# scripts/frontend-config.sh is the only reader: it canonicalises these four into JSON
# and fingerprints them, so a deploy can refuse an image built for somewhere else.
VITE_APP_ENV=production
VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER=microsoft
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api
VITE_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=119dfe0f-e3b6-487b-b695-7f0dabe327c2
VITE_MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=e76c9cee-253d-47f7-bae3-a36caaa916c1
VITE_MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI=https://evts.id.vn
